<!--
  Phase 7 - Stage 4f: tampering with the controls that PREVENT BYOVD.

  This fires one step EARLIER in the chain than the driver load. Recent
  tradecraft (documented Feb 2026) disables HVCI, the vulnerable-driver
  blocklist or Credential Guard first, then loads the driver into a system that
  no longer objects. Catching the disable is catching the attack before it has
  kernel access.

  Sensor note: sysmon-modular covers Defender's registry keys thoroughly but did
  not reference HVCI, the blocklist, or Credential Guard at all - the controls
  specific to BYOVD. Those paths were added to the RegistryEvent include list.
  Third time in this phase that the shipped config filtered exactly the
  telemetry a BYOVD detection needs: verify the sensor before writing the rule.

  Field structure confirmed from live EID 13 events:
    win.eventdata.targetObject = HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\CI\Config\VulnerableDriverBlocklistEnable
    win.eventdata.details      = the written value (DWORD 0 = control disabled)
    win.eventdata.image        = the writer

  Two findings that shape the regex:
    - Case is NOT normalised as expected ("HKLM\System", not "HKLM\SYSTEM"),
      so matching must be case-insensitive.
    - targetObject ends with the VALUE name, not the key, so the control name
      appears mid-string - anchoring on the end would never match.
    - Backslashes arrive escaped, hence [\\]+ rather than \\.
    - targetObject ends AT the value name with no trailing separator, so the
      pattern must close with ([\\]+|$) - requiring a trailing backslash means
      the rule can never match. Cost a full debugging cycle to find.

  Anchor: sysmon_event_13 (WITH the underscore before the digit, unlike
  sysmon_event6 - the naming is inconsistent across EIDs and must be read from a
  live default rule, never assumed).

  NOT 92300/92302: those were copied over from the Stage 1b persistence rules,
  but 92300 is not a generic EID 13 rule - its own pcre2 restricts it to
  CurrentVersion\Run paths. A code-integrity key never enters that branch, so
  anything inheriting from it stays silent no matter how correct its regex is.
  Verified from the shipped rule text rather than inferred.
-->
<group name="sysmon,windows,byovd,">

  <rule id="100380" level="13">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_13</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)[\\]+(DeviceGuard[\\]+Scenarios[\\]+HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity|DeviceGuard[\\]+EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity|CI[\\]+Config[\\]+VulnerableDriverBlocklistEnable|CI[\\]+Config[\\]+HVCIMATRequired)([\\]+|$)</field>
    <description>BYOVD prevention tampering: kernel code-integrity control modified - $(win.eventdata.targetObject) set to $(win.eventdata.details) by $(win.eventdata.image)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
      <id>T1553.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>impair_defenses,byovd,code_integrity,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100381" level="13">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_13</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)[\\]+(Lsa[\\]+LsaCfgFlags|Lsa[\\]+RunAsPPL|CodeIntegrity[\\]+TestSigning)([\\]+|$)</field>
    <description>Credential/signing protection tampering: $(win.eventdata.targetObject) set to $(win.eventdata.details) by $(win.eventdata.image)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
      <id>T1553.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>impair_defenses,byovd,code_integrity,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
